! mcp_bootstrap_p189.strap -- MCP printed page 189: the tail of the
! NEW-TAPE / WRITE-LABEL retry arm, the two I-O indicator aliases,
! and the whole "RETRY FOR SPACEFILE OP" routine (K K SPAC).
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.196.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 600 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LG000042   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  189".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 221415. <<<
! Six digits, read at 900 dpi, clear of the dashed rule.  That is
! LINE 1'S location WORD (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1) and
! line 1 here is a located row, which is the case that rule predicts
! to be numbered rather than blank.  Line 1's own LOCATION column
! reads 221415.40, so the page's origin word is witnessed TWICE and
! independently; only the ".40" rests on the LOCATION column alone.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS, AND HOW MANY ROWS IT HAS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-SEVEN PRINTED LINES, counted off the CARD-SERIAL column and
! not off the survey (the brief no longer quotes survey endpoints,
! and this page is a good illustration of why).  The serials run
! LG000042 on line 1 to LG000074 on line 47 with no break.  They
! are FEWER than the lines because a SIC and its branch share one
! card: lines 20 and 21 are both LG000056, lines 2 and 3 both
! LG000043, and so on.  The header's own "CARD ID LG000042" is
! line 1's serial, so nothing fell off the top; line 47 is followed
! by blank paper to the foot of the form, so nothing fell off the
! bottom.
!
!   lines  1-11   the tail of an earlier routine: the machine-error
!                 test (BZB on $R.63), K NEW T / KREE / K LABEL,
!                 and K K EXIT -- a CONNECTIVE, C0000(BU,8),, --
!                 followed by the branch to K K IND.
!   lines 12-13   TWO SYN CARDS planted in the middle of the code,
!                 aliasing the two I-O indicator cells.
!   lines 14-16   three comment cards, the middle one the banner
!                 "RETRY FOR SPACEFILE OP".
!   lines 17-47   K K SPAC, the spacefile retry: rewind (control
!                 code (8)136.), wait for end-op, clear the control
!                 word, set installation density, spaceblock
!                 ((8)76.), set file density from S F DEN, retry
!                 spacefile ((8)77.), then the three exits --
!                 K SOS SP (count-and-branch, three tries), RK1
!                 (machine error) and K K EXIT twice.
!
! THE PAGE IS CONTIGUOUS FROM 221415.40 TO 221444.00 AND HAS NO
! HOLE.  No SLC, no DR/DRZ, no VF, no XW, no reservation of any
! kind; FOUR full words (lines 1, 10, 32 and 45) and the rest half
! words.  Every arrival was checked by hand against the next
! row's printed LOCATION and all forty-six transitions agree.  The
! last row is a half-word branch at 221444.00, so the counter leaves
! this page at 221444.40.
!
! >>> FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR THE NEIGHBOURS. <<<
!   printed p.188 ends with its last emitted half word at 221415.00
!   (its counter arrives here at 221415.40).  The heading-row
!   LOCATION field corroborates the WORD independently of my reading
!   of the LOCATION column -- AND SO DOES THE VOLUME OCR OF p.188,
!   whose last listing row reads "221415.00  221405.44 48
!   CB,2,K R READ.32  -TRY KREP TIMES BEFORE GIVING UP": a half word
!   at 221415.00, which leaves the counter at exactly 221415.40.
!   That is an index to another page, so it corroborates rather than
!   proves -- but it is a reading nobody made to suit this deck.
!   printed p.190 begins at 221444.40.
!   printed p.188 is also where K IO IND (221370.00) and K K IND
!   (221371.00) should be DEFINED -- they are 21 and 20 words below
!   this page's origin, which puts them squarely in p.188's span.
!   See THE DATA DESCRIPTION, below: that page owes the image a
!   length for both.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN IS ASSERTED, NOT CHECKED -- AND I RAN THE INJECTION
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The page prints no SLC, so this deck writes a hard "SLC,
! (8)221415.40".  The brief's test for whether that origin is
! CHECKED is "does any landed deck SYN a label this page DEFINES?".
! Three do -- pp.156, 160 and 163 all carry "EIOIND SYN, (8)221370.0"
! and "EINDR SYN, (8)221371.0" -- AND IT STILL DOES NOT CHECK THE
! ORIGIN, which is the trap on this page and the reason the note is
! here rather than a one-word "CHECKED".
!
! EIOIND and EINDR are not PLACED by this page's location counter.
! They are SYN aliases whose value comes from K IO IND and K K IND,
! two symbols defined somewhere else entirely.  Moving this deck's
! SLC moves nothing they depend on.  MEASURED, by shifting the
! origin one half word and re-merging:
!
!   origin as printed    (8)221415.40  -> merge OK, 775 defined, 79
!                                       external
!   origin shifted       (8)221416.00  -> merge STILL OK, same counts
!
! The labels this page really does PLACE are KNEWT, KREE, KLABEL,
! KKEXIT, KKSPAC, RK1 and KSOSSP, and NO landed deck references any
! of them (measured: zero whole-name hits for each across decks/).
! So: >>> ORIGIN ASSERTED, FOR EXACTLY AS LONG AS IT TAKES p.188 OR
! p.190 TO LAND. <<<
!
! Three of those labels ARE referenced by the neighbours, with the
! addresses printed in their own BINARY OUTPUT columns.  From the
! volume OCR, used here as an index to OTHER pages and never as a
! witness for this one:
!
!   p.188  221372.40  221424.32 C2  BXE,K K SPAC
!   p.188  221373.40  221421.72 C2  BXE,K LABEL
!   p.188  221403.40  221422.50 00  B,K K EXIT
!   p.188  221414.40  221422.50 00  B,K K EXIT
!   p.190  221463.40  221422.50 00  B,K K EXIT
!
! 0o221424.32 is K K SPAC at 221424.00 with bit 32 clear, 0o221421.72
! is K LABEL at 221421.40, and 0o221422.50 is K K EXIT at 221422.40 --
! the three locations this deck's counter produces.  So the origin is
! CORROBORATED now by five rows on two other pages, and it becomes
! CHECKED BY THE MERGE the moment either neighbour lands.  If either
! transcriber reads a different address for any of those three
! labels, THIS DECK'S ORIGIN IS WRONG AND THE MERGE WILL SAY SO.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LINES 12 AND 13, AND THE DATA DESCRIPTION THEY PRINT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The two cards read, from my own ink at 900 dpi:
!
!   line 12  221370.00+  +00000000  BU,100,10   E IO IND  SYN,K IO IND
!   line 13  221371.00+  +00000000  BU,40 ,10   E IND R   SYN,K K IND
!
! >>> WHAT THE dds REALLY READS, SINCE IT WAS ASKED. <<<  To the eye
! at 900 dpi the two fields print "BU,1CC,1C" and "BU,4C ,1C".  Every
! one of those C's is a damaged 0, and this is the octal-field
! argument at full strength rather than a guess about shape: the dds
! field prints MODE,LENGTH,BYTESIZE in OCTAL (printed p.101 prints
! twenty-three of them, e.g. "BU,32 ,10" against the card
! "S RMTC SYN(BU,26),1.32", 0o32 = 26), so a C cannot occur in it at
! all.  The true reading is
!
!      BU,100,10   = mode BU, length 0o100 = 64 bits, byte size 8
!      BU,40 ,10   = mode BU, length 0o40  = 32 bits, byte size 8
!
! and the SAME ROW calibrates it twice over -- line 12's LOCATION
! column prints "221370.00+" with both zeros of the ".00" closed
! while the dds two inches away prints them open, so ink varies per
! character on one row, exactly as the workflow says.
!
! >>> AND THE "IO" IS CONFIRMED, NOT INHERITED. <<<  The round glyph
! in "E IO IND" / "K IO IND" prints open, and on this drum that
! carries almost no information (p.184 measured O, C and 0 as
! indistinguishable by width or by openness).  It is settled on rung
! 2 of the ladder, the TYPESET manuals, which are a different press:
!
!   whole-name counts over docs/ocr/, counting NAMES and not
!   substrings (the ZID/ZIO lesson):
!       KIOIND  11        KICIND  1
!
! and this page's line 38 prints the same two letters in a second
! name, "K IO REJ", where the manuals read KIOREJ 7 against KICREJ 3.
! Two independent names, same answer.  An earlier deck's "IC" reading
! is wrong and the correction stands.
!
! >>> THE dds IS NOT WRITTEN INTO THIS DECK, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
! <<<  Lines 12 and 13 write NO data description of their own; they
! INHERIT it from their operands (C22-6531 printed p.31, "the meaning
! of SYN is always one of exact substitution").  So this page is a
! WITNESS to the length of K IO IND and K K IND, not their authority:
!
!       K IO IND  is (BU,64)      K K IND  is (BU,32)
!
! and the page that PRINTS those defining cards -- predicted above to
! be printed p.188 -- is the authority and owes the image the dds.
!
! Writing "(BU,64)" on this deck's K IO IND stand-in would make the
! merge ABORT, not fail one row, because four landed decks (pp.155,
! 156, 163, 183) already declare it with no dds.  MEASURED, the exact
! refusal:
!
!   FAIL KIOIND is declared with more than one data description:
!   (no dds) on printed p.155, printed p.156, printed p.163, printed
!   p.183; (BU,64) on printed p.189.  A connective or a VFL operation
!   that writes no dds takes its length from the SYMBOL, so the merge
!   would silently pick one.
!
! That is the duplicate-label case in another costume -- the whole
! image checks nothing rather than one row failing -- so this deck
! matches the landed stand-ins and records the lengths here instead.
!
! >>> AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME RULE FIRED ON THIS PAGE WHILE I
! WAS WRITING IT, WHICH IS WHY THE DECISION ABOVE IS NOT LAZINESS.
! <<<  My K REP stand-in was first written "SYN, 30", dds-less, and
! the merge then refused:
!
!   FAIL KREP is declared with more than one data description: (no
!   dds) on printed p.189; (BU,18) on printed p.125.
!
! because printed p.185 PRINTS K REP's defining card, "K REP
! SYN(BU,18),30" with the dds column reading BU,22 ,10, and p.125's
! deck had just been corrected to carry it.  So where an AUTHORITY
! exists the dds goes IN (this deck now writes "KREP SYN(BU,18),30"),
! and where it does not yet exist -- K IO IND, K K IND -- writing a
! length this page only INHERITED would break the image instead of
! documenting it.  The two cases look identical and are opposite.
! >>> ACTION FOR WHOEVER LANDS p.188: declare K IO IND (BU,64) and
! K K IND (BU,32) on the defining card, and fix the five dds-less
! stand-ins (pp.155, 156, 163, 183 and this one) in the same commit.
! <<<
!
! >>> REGISTERING IT: tests/check_mcp_body_image.py's "FIRST, LAST"
! AND ITS body_page_count, not strap/mcp_image.py's MCP_IPL_PAGES.
! <<<  body_pages() builds a CONTIGUOUS range, so LAST cannot be
! moved to 189 on its own -- pp.185-188 would be looked for and do
! not exist.  The block pp.185-192 has to land together, and then
! FIRST, LAST = 99, 192 and body_page_count goes 85 -> 93 (p.189
! compiles forty-two words and is certainly not a NO_DECK_PAGES
! page; the other seven are their own transcribers' to confirm).
!
! >>> AND THE WARNING printed p.184's DECK WROTE, WHICH APPLIES
! HARDER HERE. <<<  I merged pp.99-184 + p.189, a list no invocation
! of the test can build, and I called mcp_image.build() rather than
! the test's main(), SO every_word_accounted_for NEVER RAN.  With
! only this page appended the image holds a hole from 0o221261 to
! 0o221414 -- NINETY-TWO WORDS, printed pp.185-188's -- and that hole
! is NEITHER of the two legitimate kinds: this page has no DR/DRZ and
! no forward SLC, and neither does p.184.  The image will stay green
! across a gap it should not be green across until pp.185-188 land.
!
! WHAT WAS RUN, AND AGAINST WHAT.  The merge was run by hand because
! this deck cannot edit under tests/:  pp.99-184 + this page merge
! without disagreement, 775 defined and 79 external, and p.189 becomes
! the DEFINER of EIOIND and EINDR, so pp.156, 160 and 163's three
! stand-ins for them are now checked rather than merely agreeing with
! each other.  check_symbol_spellings.py was run against the WHOLE
! landed decks/ directory plus this page (456 decks, 1554 symbol
! definitions, no symbol spelled two ways) and
! check_deck_listing_comments.py the same way (118/173 decks with a
! parseable block, 4207 rows parsed, 3099 checks compared, 0
! mismatches); this deck's own block alone parses 44 rows and compares
! 42 checks.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS: WHAT THIS PAGE SETTLED ON ITS OWN INK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE DRUM IS BAD HERE EVEN BY THIS VOLUME'S STANDARDS.  Round
! glyphs print open almost everywhere -- line 1's BINARY OUTPUT
! prints "C0CC11.77 80" for 000011.77 -- and the U of "K UNR R"
! loses its right stem and prints "K LNR R" on line 22.  That last
! one is not a judgement call: line 22 compiles 221325.40 80 and
! printed p.184 line 22 is the same card at the same address, with
! printed p.186 line 30 the defining card.
!
! RUNG 4 (the same statement twice on one page, same character
! position, open ink against closed) fires FOUR times here and is
! what settles the letters:
!
!   lines 43/44  B,K K EXIT   -- 44 prints a CLOSED E, 43 an open
!                               one.  KKEXIT settled off this page.
!   lines 28/35  B,K K DENS   -- 28 prints a D with a straight left
!                               stem and squared corners, 35 prints
!                               it open.  D settled off this page
!                               (and KKDENS has 5 manual hits).
!   lines 27/34  SIC,K DEN OK -- same, 27 closed and 34 open.
!   lines  1/45  BZB,$R.63,K SOS -- line 1's O prints CLOSED.
!
!   lines 20/30 (SIC,K SEOP R) and 21/31 (B,K K EOP) print OPEN in
!   BOTH instances, so they are consistency and not evidence; they
!   are settled below by other means.  Saying which is which is the
!   point of the rung.
!
! K K EOP is settled on rung 1, the COMMENT COLUMN: line 23's
! comment reads "-WAIT FOR END OP REW", so the letters are E-O-P in
! English on the same page.  Manuals: KKEOP 5 hits.
!
! K SEOP R and J SEOP T share four characters in the same shape.
! The manuals settle the second one outright -- JSEOPT 5 hits
! against JSECPT 1, KSEOPT 23 against KSECPT 3 -- and the first is
! the same four glyphs in another name on the same page.  The
! trailing R and T are NOT in doubt (neither letter is confusable on
! this drum).
!
! >>> A STRUCTURAL ARGUMENT I DREW AND THEN WITHDREW, because it is
! wrong and the next reader would draw it too. <<<  "K SEOP R is the
! save cell of the routine it branches to, like K UNR R for K UNR"
! does NOT hold: printed p.184 lines 22-23 are "SIC,K UNR R" /
! "B,K K LOOP", so the cell is not named after the routine, and here
! the routine is K K EOP, which would want "K K EOP R".  The trailing
! R is a listing-wide return-cell convention (K UNR R, K CBJ R,
! K SEOP R) and says nothing about the four glyphs before it.  The
! manual counts and line 40 carry this on their own.
!
! THE VOLUME OCR OF THE NEIGHBOURING PAGES BACKS IT, as an index and
! not as a witness for this page: printed p.190 prints "B,J SEOP T"
! at 221500.00 and "SIC,K SEOP R" at 221473.40, and its defining card
! at 221475.00 reads "K CTL CM  CTL(SEOP),..." -- three more
! instances on a page I did not transcribe.
!
! LINE 19's "LX,C,K CTL CM": the C after LX is an INDEX REGISTER
! NUMBER and can only be a decimal integer, so it is 0 -- and the
! compiled .00 (2J for J=0, against line 33's LV,1 printing .02)
! confirms it.  Written "LX,0,K CTL CM".
!
! LINE 10's "C0000(BU,8),,": positions 2-5 of a connective can hold
! only 0 or 1, so four round glyphs there are four zeros with
! certainty -- and unlike most octal-field arguments THIS ONE IS
! CONFIRMED BY THE COMPILED WORD, because the four bits are printed.
! Measured, varying only the connective:
!
!       C0000(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.00 70   <- the page
!       C0001(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.02 70
!       C0010(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.04 70
!       C0100(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.10 70
!       C1000(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.20 70
!       C1111(BU,8),,  ->  000000.00 80 010000.36 70
!
! so line 10's !CHECK is a READING of all four bits, not a tautology.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (A) >>> K CTL CM (line 19), AND IT IS THE WEAKEST NAME ON THE
!     PAGE. <<<  ZERO hits in any manual: `grep -ohE 'KCTL[A-Z0-9]*'`
!     over all of docs/ocr/ returns KCTL 1, KCTLC 3, KCTLE 1,
!     KCTLEC 1, KCTLUC 1 and NO KCTLCM -- and all three KCTLC hits
!     are the substring inside "RKCTLC", which is not a witness
!     (the brief's own warning).  Both round glyphs print open and
!     neither can be settled by shape, so the reading is English --
!     "control command", beside line 25's comment "-CLEAR CONTROL
!     WORD" -- which is the footing printed p.184 distrusted for
!     K BOUNC.
!     IT IS BETTER OFF THAN K BOUNC WAS, because THE DEFINING CARD IS
!     ON THE NEXT PAGE and the volume OCR indexes it: printed p.190,
!     "221475.00 ... K CTL CM  CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),0.0($2)", plus
!     three more "LX,C,K CTL CM" rows on that page at 221445.40,
!     221457.40 and 221473.00.  Same drum, same reader, so that is
!     corroboration from another PAGE and not an independent witness.
!     The VALUE is solid in the word: an LX shows bit 32 and the
!     word, so 221475 and bit-32-clear are read; the bit offset
!     inside the half word is not.
!     WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: printed p.190's transcriber RENDERING
!     that defining card.  My falsifiable claim for them: p.190 has
!     a card at 221475.00 whose name field reads K CTL CM and whose
!     statement is a CTL.
!
! (B) K SEOP R (lines 20, 30) at 221502.00 and K DEN OK (lines 27,
!     34) at 221474.40 have no manual entry of their own (KSEOPT
!     yes, KSEOPR no; KDENOK zero hits anywhere).  Both are SIC
!     operands, so the ADDRESSES are bit-exact; only the spellings
!     are open.  K DEN OK's D is settled by rung 4 on this page
!     (line 27 prints it closed, line 34 open) and its "OK" is
!     English beside line 28's K K DENS.
!     WHAT WOULD SETTLE THEM: the defining cards at 221502.00 and
!     221474.40, both on printed p.190 or p.191.
!
! (C) >>> KREE (line 6, 221420.40) RESTS ON INK ALONE AND I SAY SO.
!     <<<  It is a label this page DEFINES and that NOTHING
!     references: zero whole-name hits across decks/, and no row in
!     either volume's OCR carries 221420.40 except this page's own.
!     The four bare "KREE" hits in docs/ocr/ do NOT help, and
!     checking them is the brief's substring lesson over again --
!     `grep -n 'KREE' docs/ocr/*.txt` puts one inside "RKREE", two
!     inside hundred-character garbage runs
!     ("...RHRHRRHRKRHHHHRHHERHHRKREE") and the fourth IS printed
!     p.189's own OCR line.  No second witness of any kind.
!     WHAT IT DOES HAVE is clean ink and no confusable glyph: at
!     1800 dpi the name is K-R-E-E with both E's showing all three
!     horizontal bars, and the pairs that break on this drum are
!     O/C/0, C/D, B/8, U/L and G/Q, none of which is in it.
!     WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: any page referencing 221420.40.  It is
!     the one name on this page with nothing but the drum behind it.
!
!     K SOS SP (line 46) has zero manual hits too, but it inherits
!     the settled K SOS -- printed pp.125 and 188 both spell it --
!     and its two remaining letters are S and P.
!
! (D) THE TWO LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISKS, lines 2 (221416.40 *) and
!     29 (221432.00 *).  Recorded and nothing proposed.  Neither row
!     is an SLC, neither is a CNOP, neither starts a routine, and
!     line 2 is a SIC while line 29 is an LVI -- so they are not the
!     same kind of row as each other.  Per the brief I am NOT
!     proposing a rule from one page.
!
! (E) WHETHER THE NAME-FIELD AND OPERAND BLANKS ARE DATA.  Eight
!     names print with blanks ("K NEW T", "K LABEL", "K K EXIT",
!     "E IO IND", "E IND R", "K K SPAC", "K SOS SP") and two print
!     closed up ("KREE", "RK1") -- and line 2's name "K NEW T" is
!     referred to by line 7 as "B,KNEWT", CLOSED UP, on the same
!     page, compiling 221416.50 against line 2's 221416.40.  One
!     page prints one symbol both ways and the assembler must have
!     ignored the blank.  Nothing here says whether STRAP's own name
!     field treated it as data.
!
! (F) THE BRIEF'S OPEN QUESTION -- does a mode-only dds "(BU)"
!     inherit a LENGTH from its operand symbol? -- IS NOT TOUCHED BY
!     THIS PAGE.  No row here writes a mode-only dds.  Line 32's
!     "L(BU,1),S F DEN($13),46" writes an explicit length 1 against a
!     symbol whose own length is 2, which is the ordinary override
!     case and settles nothing about inheritance.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER: THREE DEPARTURES FROM THE PRINTED FORM, ALL FLAGGED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every statement was handed to strap_asm IN ITS PRINTED FORM first.
! Three were refused.  Two of them are the gaps the brief already
! lists; THE FIRST IS NEW and belongs in the next brief.
!
! >>> NEW GAP: A BARE NAME WITH BLANKS IS ACCEPTED AS AN INSTRUCTION
! OPERAND AND REFUSED AS A SYN OPERAND. <<<  Measured, same deck,
! same symbols:
!
!       B,K K IND          -> assembles
!       EIOIND SYN,K IO IND-> "SYN symbol(s) never resolve --
!                             undefined: EIOIND (needs 'K IO IND')"
!
! The brief documents "a blank inside a name INSIDE AN EXPRESSION is
! refused"; this is a BARE name, no expression, and the SYN path
! simply does not strip the blanks the instruction path strips.
! Written here as "SYN,KIOIND" and "SYN,KKIND", closed up.  THE
! PRINTED FORMS ARE "SYN,K IO IND" AND "SYN,K K IND".
!
! Known gap, hit once: a blank inside a name INSIDE AN EXPRESSION.
!       CB,2,K K SPAC.32   -> "undefined symbol 'K'"
!       CB,2,KKSPAC.32     -> assembles
! Line 46 is written closed up.  THE PRINTED FORM IS
! "CB,2,K K SPAC.32".
!
! Known gap, hit on every named row: a blank in the NAME field is
! refused, so all ten labels are written closed up.  The printed
! names are in the listing block below, blanks and all.
!
! Nothing else was refused -- "C0000(BU,8),," with its two empty
! operands, "BZB,$R.63,K SOS", "L(BU,1),S F DEN($13),46" with the
! named index "($13)" and the bare-decimal offset 46, "LVI,1,$+1.0",
! "LV,1,$R" and the three "(8)nnn." control codes all assembled as
! printed.
!
! $R RESOLVES TO WORD 9 and the page checks it twice through two
! different operations: lines 1 and 45 print "000011.77" for the bit
! address $R.63 (0o11 = 9, 0o77 = 63) and line 33 prints "000011.02"
! for "LV,1,$R".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE EXTERNALS, AND WHICH ONES ARE READ TO THE BIT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! A SIC and a VFL operand carry the full 24-bit bit address, so those
! rows READ their operand.  A plain B and an LX/LV/LVI spend the low
! bits on their own fields and show only bit 32.  Marked below.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

KSOS    SYN, (8)221274.0             ! lines 1/45/47; landed p.125.  B + BZB -> half word only
JNEWT   SYN, (8)221614.0             ! line  2 SIC -- BIT-EXACT
JNEWTE  SYN, (8)221541.0             ! line  3 B; bit offset unobservable
KRNEW   SYN, (8)221406.40            ! line  5 B; .50 = 0o40 OR 0o10, so bit 32 IS read.  printed p.188 defines it at 221406.40
KRREAD  SYN, (8)221405.0             ! line  6 CB; bit offset unobservable.  printed p.188 defines it at 221405.00, name "K R READ"
JWRITX  SYN, (8)221660.0             ! line  8 SIC -- BIT-EXACT
JWRITL  SYN, (8)221622.0             ! line  9 B; bit offset unobservable
